  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0125 database contains 16 tables that represent various entities and relationships in a video rental store. The tables are:\n1. actor: This table contains information about the actors in the video rental store, including their first and last names, and a unique identifier for each actor.\n2. address: This table contains information about the addresses of the video rental store, including the address, address2, district, city, postal code, and phone number.\n3. category: This table contains information about the categories of films available in the video rental store, including the name of each category.\n4. city: This table contains information about the cities in which the video rental store is located, including the city name and country.\n5. country: This table contains information about the countries in which the video rental store is located, including the country name.\n6. customer: This table contains information about the customers of the video rental store, including their first and last names, email address, address, and active status.\n7. film: This table contains information about the films available in the video rental store, including the title, description, release year, language, original language, rental duration, rental rate, length, replacement cost, and rating.\n8. film_actor: This table contains information about the actors in each film, including the film ID and actor ID.\n9. film_category: This table contains information about the categories of each film, including the film ID and category ID.\n10. film_text: This table contains information about the text of each film, including the title and description.\n11. inventory: This table contains information about the inventory of films available in the video rental store, including the film ID, store ID, and last update date.\n12. language: This table contains information about the languages in which films are available in the video rental store, including the language name.\n13. payment: This table contains information about the payments made by customers, including the payment ID, customer ID, staff ID, rental ID, amount, payment date, and last update date.\n14. rental: This table contains information about the rentals made by customers, including the rental ID, rental date, inventory ID, customer ID, return date, staff ID, and last update date.\n15. staff: This table contains information about the staff members of the video rental store, including their first and last names, address ID, email, store ID, active status, username, and password.\n16. store: This table contains information about the video rental store, including the store ID, manager staff ID, address ID, and last update date.\nEach table has its own set of columns, with some tables having foreign key relationships with other tables. For example, the film_actor table has a foreign key relationship with the actor and film tables, and the rental table has a foreign key relationship with the customer, inventory, and staff tables. The database also has various constraints, such as primary keys, foreign keys, and unique constraints, to ensure data integrity and consistency.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThis project involves the Sakila test database, a fictional and simplified database designed to manage a video rental store. It contains data structures relevant to tracking movies, customers, rentals, and payments in a video rental business context.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe objective is to create a database system that efficiently manages various aspects of a video rental business, including movie information, inventory management, customer records, rental transactions, and related payments.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Store Managers: Oversee store operations and manage inventory.\n- Staff: Handle customer interactions, process rentals, and manage transactions.\n- Customers: Rent movies and interact with store services.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. Movie Management:\n   - Store and manage details about films, including titles, descriptions, release years, languages, ratings, and special features.\n   - Categorize films into different genres using a category system.\n\n2. Inventory Management:\n   - Track inventory of films across different stores.\n\n3. Customer Management:\n   - Maintain records of customer details including addresses, contact information, and rental history.\n   - Manage active status of customers.\n\n4. Rental Processing:\n   - Record rental transactions including rental dates and return dates.\n   - Associate each rental with a customer and store staff member.\n\n5. Payment Processing:\n   - Process payments linked to rental transactions.\n   - Store details of payment amounts and dates.\n\n6. Staff Management:\n   - Manage staff details for operational purposes.\n   - Assign staff members to stores.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Database Integrity: Ensure referential integrity through the use of foreign keys.\n- User Access Control: Allow different levels of access for managers, staff, and customers.\n- Scalability: Support potential increase in data and user interactions.\n- Data Consistency: Maintain data accuracy with constraints and default values.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0125.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0125\nCREATE TABLE `actor` (\n  `actor_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`actor_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `address` (\n  `address_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address2` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `district` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `city_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `postal_code` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `phone` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`address_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`city_id`) REFERENCES `city` (`city_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `category` (\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`category_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `city` (\n  `city_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `city` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `country_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`city_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`country_id`) REFERENCES `country` (`country_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `country` (\n  `country_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `country` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`country_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `customer` (\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  PRIMARY KEY (`customer_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`address_id`) REFERENCES `address` (`address_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `film` (\n  `film_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `release_year` year DEFAULT NULL,\n  `language_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `original_language_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `rental_duration` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT '3',\n  `rental_rate` decimal(5,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT '4.99',\n  `length` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `replacement_cost` decimal(5,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT '19.99',\n  `rating` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`film_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`language_id`) REFERENCES `language` (`language_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`original_language_id`) REFERENCES `language` (`language_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `film_actor` (\n  `film_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `actor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`film_id`, `actor_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`film_id`) REFERENCES `film` (`film_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`actor_id`) REFERENCES `actor` (`actor_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `film_category` (\n  `film_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`film_id`, `category_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`film_id`) REFERENCES `film` (`film_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `category` (`category_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `film_text` (\n  `film_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`film_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`film_id`) REFERENCES `film` (`film_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `inventory` (\n  `inventory_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `film_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `store_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`inventory_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`film_id`) REFERENCES `film` (`film_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`store_id`) REFERENCES `store` (`store_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `language` (\n  `language_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`language_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `payment` (\n  `payment_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `staff_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `rental_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(5,2) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_date` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`payment_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`staff_id`) REFERENCES `staff` (`staff_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`rental_id`) REFERENCES `rental` (`rental_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `rental` (\n  `rental_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `rental_date` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `inventory_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `return_date` timestamp DEFAULT NULL,\n  `staff_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`rental_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`inventory_id`) REFERENCES `inventory` (`inventory_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`staff_id`) REFERENCES `staff` (`staff_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `staff` (\n  `staff_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `store_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`staff_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`address_id`) REFERENCES `address` (`address_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`store_id`) REFERENCES `store` (`store_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `store` (\n  `store_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `manager_staff_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `address_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`store_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`manager_staff_id`) REFERENCES `staff` (`staff_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`address_id`) REFERENCES `address` (`address_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
